        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>Can a folding treadmill have auto incline?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Yes. Five of the six machines on this page raise the deck with a motor, from buttons on the console or handrails, and fold with a soft-drop or hydraulic mechanism. The incline motor sits at the front, so it stays on the floor when the deck folds.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Which folding treadmill has the most incline?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Of the machines here, the Goplus 4.75HP and Sunny’s fully assembled Auto Incline Treadmill reach 15%, and the Merach offers 18 incline levels, though Merach says its 55-inch version may top out slightly lower than the shorter model. For 30% or more, you need a non-folding incline treadmill.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is a folding treadmill with incline stable enough to run on?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              A well-built one is, within its limits. Look for a top speed of 10 to 12 mph, a belt of at least 50 to 55 inches and a user rating comfortably above your weight. The Sunny SF-T723016, Goplus 4.75HP and NordicTrack T Series 10 all reach 12 mph.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What is the difference between manual and auto incline?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Auto incline uses a motor to change the gradient while you walk and lets programs change it for you. Manual incline is set by hand, with the treadmill stopped, to one of a few positions. Manual is cheaper and simpler; auto is far more likely to get used.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How heavy are folding treadmills with incline?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Amazon’s product data puts the machines on this page at roughly 100 to 245 lb. You lift only the deck when you fold, and a soft-drop mechanism lowers it, but heavier machines are harder to fold and move.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Do I need a high ceiling for an incline treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              You need more room than for a flat treadmill. Add the deck height, the extra rise at full incline where you stand, your height and a few inches for bounce. Low basements and attics are where incline treadmills most often fail this check.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

